Yes. For **AxonTimeHR**, after Phase 5, Phase 6 should focus on **Leave Management + Holiday Management + Attendance Regularization + Approval Workflows**.

The key design principle is that Phase 6 should consume the employee, attendance, shift, and policy foundations from Phases 3–5 rather than creating parallel systems.

# PHASE 6 — LEAVE, HOLIDAY, REGULARIZATION & APPROVAL WORKFLOW DEVELOPMENT

# AXONTIMEHR — PRODUCTION-GRADE LEAVE & ATTENDANCE WORKFLOW ENGINE

---

# 1. PROJECT CONTEXT

You are continuing development of **AxonTimeHR**, a modern, advanced, production-ready, multi-tenant **Attendance + HRMS SaaS platform**.

AxonTimeHR is being developed incrementally in phases.

Previously completed:

```text
PHASE 1
Foundation
```

```text
PHASE 2
Authentication + Identity + RBAC
```

```text
PHASE 3
Organization + Employee Management
```

```text
PHASE 4
Attendance + Shift Management
```

```text
PHASE 5
GPS + Geofencing + Verified Attendance
```

Phase 6 must build on these existing modules.

---

# 2. PHASE 6 OBJECTIVE

The objective of Phase 6 is to implement a complete:

# LEAVE + HOLIDAY + ATTENDANCE REGULARIZATION + APPROVAL WORKFLOW ENGINE

The module must support:

* Leave types
* Leave policies
* Leave balances
* Leave accrual
* Leave requests
* Leave cancellation
* Leave approval
* Leave rejection
* Leave withdrawal
* Half-day leave
* Partial-day leave where applicable
* Multiple leave types
* Holiday calendars
* Branch-specific holidays
* Organization holidays
* Weekly offs
* Leave validation against attendance
* Attendance regularization
* Manager approval
* HR approval
* Multi-level approval workflows
* Delegated approvals
* Approval history
* Notifications foundation
* Audit logs
* Tenant isolation
* RBAC

The final system should support both:

```text
Employee Self Service
```

and:

```text
Manager / HR / Admin workflows
```

---

# 3. CRITICAL DEVELOPMENT RULE

Before writing code:

**INSPECT THE ENTIRE EXISTING AXONTIMEHR REPOSITORY.**

Do not recreate:

* Employee model
* Organization model
* Branch model
* Department model
* Attendance model
* Shift model
* Authentication
* JWT
* RBAC
* Tenant handling
* Audit logging
* Existing notification architecture
* Database connection
* API architecture
* Frontend design system

Reuse existing functionality.

If an existing table/model/service already supports part of Phase 6, extend it rather than creating a duplicate.

---

# 4. FIRST STEP — CODEBASE ANALYSIS

Before implementation inspect:

```text
Backend
Frontend
PostgreSQL models
Migrations
Employee module
Attendance module
Shift module
Location module
Attendance policy
RBAC
Tenant middleware
Audit logging
Notification foundation
API patterns
Testing framework
Docker configuration
Environment configuration
```

Then produce an implementation analysis:

```text
1. Existing architecture
2. Existing employee relationships
3. Existing attendance relationships
4. Existing shift architecture
5. Existing holiday implementation, if any
6. Existing RBAC
7. Existing approval-related code
8. Existing notification infrastructure
9. Existing audit infrastructure
10. Database changes required
11. API changes required
12. Frontend changes required
13. Migration strategy
14. Testing strategy
```

Only after this analysis begin implementation.

---

# 5. HIGH-LEVEL PHASE 6 ARCHITECTURE

Use a reusable workflow-oriented architecture:

```text
                    AxonTimeHR
                        |
                Authentication
                        |
                   Tenant Context
                        |
              Leave / Workflow APIs
                        |
                 Service Layer
                        |
        +---------------+---------------+
        |               |               |
   Leave Engine   Approval Engine   Holiday Engine
        |               |               |
        +---------------+---------------+
                        |
                 Attendance Engine
                        |
                    PostgreSQL
```

Supporting systems:

```text
Audit
Notifications
Redis/Celery
Object Storage where required
```

---

# 6. DOMAIN MODULES

Phase 6 should contain the following logical modules:

```text
Leave Management
Holiday Management
Leave Balance
Leave Policy
Leave Accrual
Leave Request
Leave Approval
Leave Cancellation
Attendance Regularization
Approval Workflow
Approval History
Delegation
Notifications Foundation
```

Keep modules loosely coupled.

---

# 7. LEAVE TYPE MANAGEMENT

Create a configurable leave type master.

Examples:

```text
CASUAL_LEAVE
SICK_LEAVE
EARNED_LEAVE
ANNUAL_LEAVE
PRIVILEGE_LEAVE
MATERNITY_LEAVE
PATERNITY_LEAVE
UNPAID_LEAVE
COMP_OFF
```

Do not hard-code business-specific leave types into application logic.

Each tenant should be able to configure appropriate leave types.

---

# 8. LEAVE TYPE DATA MODEL

Potential fields:

```text
id
tenant_id
code
name
description
color/reference for UI
paid
requires_document
allow_half_day
allow_partial_day
allow_backdated
allow_future
minimum_notice_days
maximum_consecutive_days
status
created_at
updated_at
created_by
updated_by
```

Use the existing naming conventions.

Do not add fields without business value.

---

# 9. LEAVE POLICY

Create a configurable leave policy.

Possible configuration:

```text
leave_type
annual_entitlement
accrual_frequency
accrual_amount
carry_forward_allowed
carry_forward_limit
encashment_allowed
maximum_balance
negative_balance_allowed
probation_allowed
notice_period
document_required
approval_required
```

The policy must be tenant-specific.

---

# 10. LEAVE POLICY ASSIGNMENT

Allow policies to be assigned based on appropriate organizational rules.

Potential scopes:

```text
Organization
Branch
Department
Employee
Employee Group
Employment Type
Designation
```

Define a deterministic precedence rule.

For example:

```text
Employee-specific policy
        ↓
Employee-group policy
        ↓
Department policy
        ↓
Branch policy
        ↓
Organization default
```

Document and test the precedence.

---

# 11. LEAVE BALANCE

Implement employee leave balances.

Example:

```text
Employee:
Neeraj

Casual Leave:
Allocated = 12
Used = 4
Pending = 2
Available = 6
```

The balance model should support:

```text
opening_balance
accrued
adjusted
used
pending
expired
carried_forward
available
```

Avoid storing derived values unnecessarily if they can be safely calculated.

If balances are materialized for performance, define the authoritative source clearly.

---

# 12. LEAVE LEDGER

Prefer a ledger-based architecture for balance changes.

Example:

```text
Leave Ledger

Opening       +12
Accrual        +1
Leave Used     -2
Adjustment     +1
Expiry         -1
------------------
Available      11
```

This makes the system:

* Auditable
* Reversible
* Traceable
* Easier to reconcile

Do not simply overwrite a balance number without recording why it changed.

---

# 13. LEAVE BALANCE TRANSACTIONS

Possible transaction types:

```text
OPENING_BALANCE
ACCRUAL
LEAVE_DEBIT
LEAVE_REVERSAL
MANUAL_ADJUSTMENT
CARRY_FORWARD
EXPIRY
ENCASHMENT
```

Every balance-changing operation should generate an auditable transaction.

---

# 14. LEAVE ACCRUAL

Support configurable accrual rules.

Examples:

```text
Monthly:
1 day/month

Quarterly:
3 days/quarter

Yearly:
24 days/year
```

Future-compatible accrual rules may include:

```text
joining-date based
calendar-year based
financial-year based
monthly pro-rata
```

Do not hard-code one accrual model.

---

# 15. PRO-RATA ACCRUAL

Prepare support for employees joining mid-year.

Example:

```text
Annual entitlement = 24 days
Joining date = July
```

The system may calculate a proportional entitlement according to tenant policy.

Make the formula configurable and clearly documented.

---

# 16. LEAVE YEAR

Support configurable leave years.

Examples:

```text
January → December
April → March
```

Do not hard-code January 1 as the beginning of every leave year.

The leave-year calculation must respect tenant configuration.

---

# 17. CARRY FORWARD

Support carry-forward policies.

Example:

```text
Annual Leave = 20
Carry Forward Limit = 5
```

At year end:

```text
Unused = 8
Carry Forward = 5
Expired = 3
```

Record the operation in the leave ledger.

---

# 18. LEAVE EXPIRY

Support expiration rules.

Examples:

```text
End of Leave Year
Specific Date
Never Expires
```

When leave expires:

```text
Leave Balance
      ↓
Expiry Transaction
      ↓
Audit
```

Do not silently delete unused balance.

---

# 19. NEGATIVE BALANCE

Support tenant-configurable behavior:

```text
ALLOW
DO_NOT_ALLOW
ALLOW_WITH_APPROVAL
```

If negative balance is allowed, define the maximum negative amount.

---

# 20. LEAVE REQUEST

Implement employee leave requests.

Example:

```http
POST /api/v1/leaves/requests
```

Request:

```json
{
  "leave_type_id": "...",
  "start_date": "2026-09-10",
  "end_date": "2026-09-12",
  "duration": "FULL_DAY",
  "reason": "Personal work"
}
```

The server must calculate the final number of leave days.

Do not trust:

```text
total_days
```

from the client.

---

# 21. LEAVE DURATION

Support:

```text
FULL_DAY
HALF_DAY
PARTIAL_DAY
```

Where policy allows.

For half-day leave support:

```text
FIRST_HALF
SECOND_HALF
```

Do not allow half-day leave for leave types that prohibit it.

---

# 22. WORKING-DAY CALCULATION

Leave duration must consider:

```text
Working days
Weekly offs
Holidays
Employee schedule
Branch calendar
```

Example:

```text
Monday = Working
Tuesday = Working
Wednesday = Holiday
Thursday = Working
Friday = Working
Saturday = Off
Sunday = Off
```

A leave request across these dates should count only applicable leave days according to policy.

Do not simply calculate:

```text
end_date - start_date + 1
```

---

# 23. HOLIDAY INTEGRATION

Leave calculation must integrate with Phase 6 holiday calendars.

Example:

```text
Leave:
Monday → Friday

Wednesday:
Holiday

Result:
4 leave days
```

unless tenant policy explicitly counts holidays as leave.

Make this behavior configurable.

---

# 24. WEEKLY OFF INTEGRATION

Do not count scheduled weekly offs as leave by default.

Use employee/tenant work-week rules established in Phase 4.

---

# 25. LEAVE OVERLAP VALIDATION

Prevent invalid overlapping leave.

Example:

Existing:

```text
10 Sep → 12 Sep
```

New:

```text
11 Sep → 15 Sep
```

must be detected as overlapping.

The server must reject or handle it according to policy.

---

# 26. PENDING LEAVE OVERLAP

Also determine whether pending requests should block new requests.

Configuration may be:

```text
BLOCK_OVERLAP
ALLOW_OVERLAP
```

Recommended default:

```text
BLOCK_OVERLAP
```

---

# 27. LEAVE STATUS

Use controlled statuses.

Example:

```text
DRAFT
PENDING
APPROVED
REJECTED
CANCEL_REQUESTED
CANCELLED
WITHDRAWN
EXPIRED
```

Define valid transitions.

---

# 28. LEAVE STATE MACHINE

Implement/document:

```text
DRAFT
  |
  ↓
PENDING
  |
  +----> REJECTED
  |
  ↓
APPROVED
  |
  +----> CANCEL_REQUESTED
  |             |
  |             ↓
  |         CANCELLED
  |
  ↓
COMPLETED
```

Employee withdrawal should only be allowed at appropriate states.

Invalid transitions must be rejected.

---

# 29. APPROVAL WORKFLOW ENGINE

Create a reusable approval workflow rather than hard-coding:

```text
Employee → Manager
```

The workflow engine should support:

```text
Single-level approval
Multi-level approval
Manager approval
HR approval
Admin approval
Conditional approval
```

Future modules should be able to reuse it.

---

# 30. WORKFLOW EXAMPLE

Example:

```text
Employee
   |
   ↓
Leave Request
   |
   ↓
Reporting Manager
   |
   ↓
HR
   |
   ↓
Approved
```

Another tenant:

```text
Employee
   |
   ↓
Manager
   |
   ↓
Approved
```

Do not force every tenant into the same approval chain.

---

# 31. APPROVAL WORKFLOW MODEL

Possible entities:

```text
ApprovalWorkflow
ApprovalWorkflowStep
ApprovalInstance
ApprovalInstanceStep
ApprovalAction
ApprovalDelegation
```

Use existing workflow models if already available.

---

# 32. APPROVAL STEP

Potential fields:

```text
step_order
approver_type
approver_reference
required
minimum_approvals
timeout
auto_approval_rules
```

Approver types may include:

```text
REPORTING_MANAGER
DEPARTMENT_HEAD
HR
ROLE
SPECIFIC_USER
ADMIN
```

---

# 33. APPROVAL INSTANCE

When a leave request is submitted:

```text
Leave Request
      ↓
Approval Workflow Resolved
      ↓
Approval Instance Created
      ↓
Current Step = Manager
```

Do not dynamically change the approval chain after approval has started unless a controlled workflow rule explicitly allows it.

---

# 34. APPROVAL ACTIONS

Support:

```text
APPROVE
REJECT
RETURN
CANCEL
DELEGATE
```

Require rejection reason where appropriate.

---

# 35. APPROVAL HISTORY

Every approval action must be recorded.

Example:

```text
Employee submitted
↓
Manager approved
↓
HR rejected
```

Record:

```text
actor
action
timestamp
comments
step
previous_status
new_status
```

Approval history must be immutable.

---

# 36. MANAGER APPROVAL

Managers should see requests only for employees they are authorized to manage.

Do not expose every tenant leave request to every manager.

Use the reporting hierarchy from Phase 3.

---

# 37. HR APPROVAL

HR permissions should allow appropriate tenant-wide or organizational visibility based on RBAC.

Do not simply grant all HR users unrestricted access without considering branch/organization scope.

---

# 38. ADMIN APPROVAL

Administrators may be able to:

* approve
* reject
* cancel
* modify
* override

But overrides must always be audited.

---

# 39. APPROVAL DELEGATION

Prepare support for:

```text
Manager unavailable
      ↓
Delegate approval to another authorized user
```

Delegation should contain:

```text
delegator
delegate
start_date
end_date
scope
status
```

Do not allow delegation to arbitrary users without authorization.

---

# 40. SELF-APPROVAL PREVENTION

An employee must not approve their own request.

Example:

```text
Employee = Neeraj
Approver = Neeraj
```

must be rejected unless an explicitly authorized emergency workflow permits it.

---

# 41. APPROVER VALIDATION

At approval time verify:

```text
Approver is authenticated
Approver belongs to tenant
Approver has permission
Approver is authorized for employee/request
Workflow step is active
Request is still pending
```

Do not trust an approver ID from the client.

---

# 42. RACE CONDITION IN APPROVAL

Two approvers may attempt to approve/reject simultaneously.

The system must prevent inconsistent state.

Example:

```text
Manager A → APPROVE
Manager B → REJECT
```

The final state must follow workflow rules and transactional locking/versioning.

---

# 43. LEAVE CANCELLATION

Support cancellation after approval where policy allows.

Example:

```text
APPROVED
   ↓
Cancellation Request
   ↓
Manager Approval
   ↓
CANCELLED
```

When cancelled:

```text
Leave Debit
     ↓
Reversal
     ↓
Balance Restored
```

The reversal must be recorded in the leave ledger.

---

# 44. PARTIAL CANCELLATION

Prepare architecture for future partial cancellation.

Example:

```text
Leave:
10 Sep → 15 Sep

Employee cancels:
13 Sep → 15 Sep
```

If not implemented now, do not design the data model in a way that makes it impossible later.

---

# 45. BACKDATED LEAVE

Support configurable policy:

```text
ALLOW
DENY
REQUIRE_APPROVAL
```

Example:

Employee requests leave for yesterday.

System evaluates:

```text
Policy
+
Attendance
+
Approval
```

---

# 46. FUTURE-DATED LEAVE

Allow leave requests for future dates subject to:

* notice period
* leave balance
* policy
* blackout dates if configured

---

# 47. BLACKOUT DATES

Prepare optional architecture for restricted periods.

Example:

```text
Year-end closing
Festival season
Critical project period
```

Possible rule:

```text
Leave not allowed
Leave requires HR approval
```

Do not hard-code blackout dates.

---

# 48. LEAVE DOCUMENTS

Some leave types may require documentation.

Example:

```text
Medical Leave
```

Create secure document metadata architecture.

Do not store arbitrary files directly in PostgreSQL.

Use secure object storage architecture where available.

---

# 49. DOCUMENT VALIDATION

Validate:

```text
file size
file type
extension
MIME
storage reference
```

Do not trust client-provided MIME information.

---

# 50. HOLIDAY MANAGEMENT

Implement complete holiday calendar management.

Support:

```text
Organization holidays
Branch holidays
Optional holidays
Restricted holidays
Employee-specific holiday calendars
```

---

# 51. HOLIDAY MODEL

Potential fields:

```text
id
tenant_id
calendar_id
holiday_date
holiday_name
holiday_type
branch_id
optional
description
status
created_at
updated_at
```

---

# 52. HOLIDAY CALENDAR

Create holiday calendars.

Example:

```text
India National Holidays 2026
Gurugram Office Holidays 2026
Bangalore Office Holidays 2026
```

A calendar can be assigned to:

```text
Organization
Branch
Employee group
Employee
```

---

# 53. HOLIDAY CONFLICT

Prevent duplicate holidays for the same effective scope.

Example:

```text
Same branch
Same date
Same calendar
```

must not accidentally create duplicate records.

---

# 54. OPTIONAL HOLIDAY

Support optional/floating holidays.

Example:

```text
Employee may select:
Diwali
or
Christmas
```

Create appropriate selection/request architecture.

---

# 55. HOLIDAY WORKED DAY

Integrate with attendance.

If employee works on a holiday:

```text
Holiday
+
Attendance
```

should be preserved for future:

```text
Overtime
Comp-off
Payroll
```

Do not automatically convert it into overtime in Phase 6 unless business rules explicitly require it.

---

# 56. ATTENDANCE REGULARIZATION

Extend Phase 4 regularization foundation into a complete workflow.

Examples:

```text
Forgot Check-in
Forgot Check-out
Wrong Check-in Time
Wrong Check-out Time
Missing Attendance
Wrong Attendance Status
```

---

# 57. REGULARIZATION REQUEST

Example:

```http
POST /api/v1/attendance/regularization
```

Request:

```json
{
  "attendance_date": "2026-09-01",
  "requested_check_in": "09:05",
  "requested_check_out": "18:10",
  "reason": "Forgot to check out"
}
```

Server calculates:

```text
worked_minutes
late
early_exit
attendance_status
```

Do not trust client-calculated attendance values.

---

# 58. REGULARIZATION APPROVAL

Flow:

```text
Employee
   ↓
Regularization Request
   ↓
Manager
   ↓
Approved
   ↓
Attendance Updated
```

If rejected:

```text
Attendance remains unchanged
```

---

# 59. REGULARIZATION AUDIT

Never silently modify attendance.

Store:

```text
Original Check-in
Original Check-out

Requested Check-in
Requested Check-out

Approved Check-in
Approved Check-out

Reason
Approver
Timestamp
```

---

# 60. ATTENDANCE IMMUTABILITY

Important attendance records should be treated as historical records.

Corrections must occur through controlled workflows.

Do not allow normal employees to directly update:

```text
attendance_status
worked_minutes
late_minutes
check_in
check_out
```

through arbitrary update APIs.

---

# 61. LEAVE ↔ ATTENDANCE INTEGRATION

When leave is approved:

```text
Leave Approved
      ↓
Attendance Calendar
      ↓
ON_LEAVE
```

Do not create duplicate attendance records unnecessarily.

Use a clear source of truth.

---

# 62. LEAVE AND ATTENDANCE CONFLICT

If employee has:

```text
Approved Leave
+
Attendance Check-in
```

define business behavior.

Possible:

```text
ALLOW
FLAG_EXCEPTION
REQUIRE_ADMIN_REVIEW
```

Recommended default:

```text
FLAG_EXCEPTION
```

rather than silently deleting attendance.

---

# 63. HALF-DAY LEAVE + ATTENDANCE

Example:

```text
Morning Half-Day Leave
+
Afternoon Attendance
```

The system should be able to represent:

```text
Half-day leave
+
Half-day work
```

without corrupting attendance calculations.

---

# 64. LEAVE BALANCE VALIDATION

When submitting a request:

```text
Available balance
+
Pending requests
+
Requested duration
```

must be evaluated according to policy.

Prevent double-spending of the same leave balance.

---

# 65. TRANSACTIONAL LEAVE APPROVAL

When leave is approved:

```text
BEGIN

Validate request
Validate balance
Update leave status
Create ledger transaction
Update attendance/calendar state
Create audit record

COMMIT
```

If any critical operation fails:

```text
ROLLBACK
```

---

# 66. BALANCE RACE CONDITION

Two leave requests must not consume the same available balance incorrectly.

Example:

```text
Available = 2

Request A = 2 days
Request B = 2 days
```

The database/service layer must prevent both from being approved if policy does not allow negative balance.

---

# 67. LEAVE BALANCE RESERVATION

Consider pending leave as:

```text
Allocated
-
Approved
-
Pending
```

where the policy requires reservation.

This prevents employees from submitting many simultaneous requests against the same balance.

---

# 68. LEAVE CALENDAR

Create employee leave calendar UI.

Display:

```text
Approved Leave
Pending Leave
Rejected Leave
Holidays
Week Off
Attendance
```

Use different visual states.

---

# 69. EMPLOYEE LEAVE DASHBOARD

Create:

```text
My Leave
```

Display:

```text
Casual Leave
Available: 6
Pending: 2

Sick Leave
Available: 8

Annual Leave
Available: 14
```

Actions:

```text
Apply Leave
View History
Cancel Leave
View Balance
```

---

# 70. APPLY LEAVE UI

Form:

```text
Leave Type
From Date
To Date
Duration
Half Day
Reason
Attachment
```

Show calculated:

```text
Requested Days: 3
Working Days: 3
Holidays: 1
Weekly Off: 1
```

The final server calculation remains authoritative.

---

# 71. MANAGER LEAVE DASHBOARD

Manager should see:

```text
Pending Requests
Approved
Rejected
Upcoming Leave
Team Calendar
```

Filters:

```text
Employee
Department
Date
Leave Type
Status
```

---

# 72. HR LEAVE DASHBOARD

HR should see organization/authorized scope statistics:

```text
Total Leave
Pending
Approved
Rejected
Employees on Leave Today
Upcoming Leave
```

---

# 73. HOLIDAY UI

Create:

```text
Holiday Calendars
```

Features:

```text
Create Calendar
Add Holiday
Edit Holiday
Remove/Deactivate Holiday
Assign Calendar
```

---

# 74. ADMIN LEAVE SETTINGS

Create:

```text
Leave Settings
```

Configure:

```text
Leave Types
Leave Policies
Accrual
Carry Forward
Expiry
Negative Balance
Notice Period
Approval Workflow
Document Requirements
```

---

# 75. WORKFLOW CONFIGURATION UI

Provide a reusable workflow editor.

Example:

```text
Leave Request

Step 1:
Reporting Manager

Step 2:
HR

Final:
Approved
```

Allow authorized administrators to configure workflow steps.

Do not make the UI unnecessarily complex in the first implementation.

---

# 76. NOTIFICATIONS FOUNDATION

Integrate with existing notification architecture.

Events:

```text
Leave Submitted
Leave Approved
Leave Rejected
Leave Cancelled
Regularization Submitted
Regularization Approved
Regularization Rejected
Approval Pending
```

Possible channels:

```text
In-App
Email
Push
SMS
WhatsApp
```

Only implement channels already supported by the existing system.

Prepare interfaces for future channels.

---

# 77. CELERY / REDIS

If Redis/Celery exists:

Use asynchronous jobs for:

```text
Leave accrual
Leave expiry
Reminder notifications
Pending approval reminders
Holiday notifications
Daily leave calendar generation
```

Do not make synchronous leave submission depend on a background job.

---

# 78. SCHEDULED JOBS

Prepare jobs such as:

```text
monthly_leave_accrual
leave_expiry_processing
approval_reminders
upcoming_leave_notifications
```

Jobs must be:

* idempotent
* retry-safe
* tenant-aware
* auditable

---

# 79. JOB IDEMPOTENCY

A monthly accrual job must not grant leave twice if retried.

Example:

```text
September accrual
```

must be uniquely identifiable.

Use ledger transaction uniqueness/idempotency keys.

---

# 80. TENANT ISOLATION

Every Phase 6 entity must belong to the correct tenant.

Tenant A must never access:

```text
Tenant B leave
Tenant B balances
Tenant B holiday calendar
Tenant B approval workflow
Tenant B regularization
```

Test this aggressively.

---

# 81. RBAC

Add permissions such as:

```text
leave.view
leave.create
leave.update
leave.cancel
leave.approve
leave.reject

leave_type.view
leave_type.create
leave_type.update
leave_type.delete

leave_policy.view
leave_policy.create
leave_policy.update

leave_balance.view
leave_balance.adjust

holiday.view
holiday.create
holiday.update
holiday.delete

regularization.view
regularization.create
regularization.approve
regularization.reject

workflow.view
workflow.create
workflow.update
workflow.delete
```

Use the existing Phase 2 RBAC mechanism.

---

# 82. DATA SCOPE

Do not rely only on role names.

Authorization should consider:

```text
Tenant
Role
Permission
Employee relationship
Branch
Department
Workflow step
```

Example:

A manager may approve employees who report to them, but not unrelated employees.

---

# 83. IDOR PROTECTION

Test malicious requests such as:

```text
GET /api/v1/leaves/OTHER_EMPLOYEE_ID
```

or:

```json
{
  "employee_id": "OTHER_EMPLOYEE"
}
```

The backend must validate authorization.

---

# 84. MASS ASSIGNMENT PROTECTION

Clients must not directly set:

```text
tenant_id
employee_id
approved_by
approval_status
balance
created_by
updated_by
```

unless explicitly authorized.

---

# 85. DATABASE DESIGN

Potential tables:

```text
leave_types
leave_policies
leave_policy_assignments
leave_balances
leave_ledger
leave_requests
leave_request_days
holiday_calendars
holidays
holiday_calendar_assignments
approval_workflows
approval_workflow_steps
approval_instances
approval_instance_steps
approval_actions
approval_delegations
regularization_requests
```

Do not blindly create all tables.

Reuse existing structures where appropriate.

---

# 86. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS

Use:

```text
Foreign Keys
Unique Constraints
Check Constraints
Tenant Constraints
Status Constraints
Date Constraints
```

Examples:

```text
leave_start <= leave_end
```

and:

```text
balance transaction belongs to same tenant as employee
```

---

# 87. DATABASE INDEXES

Important query patterns:

```text
tenant_id
employee_id
leave_type_id
status
start_date
end_date
holiday_date
workflow_id
approval_instance_id
created_at
```

Composite indexes where appropriate:

```text
tenant_id + employee_id + status
tenant_id + start_date
tenant_id + holiday_date
tenant_id + status + created_at
```

Do not create indexes blindly.

---

# 88. CONCURRENCY

Protect concurrent operations:

```text
Leave approval
Leave cancellation
Balance adjustment
Leave accrual
Regularization approval
```

Use transactions/locking/versioning where required.

---

# 89. AUDIT LOGGING

Audit:

```text
Leave Type Created
Leave Policy Changed
Leave Balance Adjusted
Leave Submitted
Leave Approved
Leave Rejected
Leave Cancelled
Holiday Created
Holiday Updated
Regularization Submitted
Regularization Approved
Regularization Rejected
Workflow Changed
Approval Delegated
```

Record:

```text
actor
tenant
entity
entity_id
action
timestamp
reason
before
after
```

Never expose sensitive internal data unnecessarily.

---

# 90. LEAVE BALANCE AUDIT

Every manual adjustment must include:

```text
Previous Balance
Adjustment
New Balance
Reason
Actor
Timestamp
```

No silent balance modifications.

---

# 91. TIMEZONE

Leave and holiday dates are generally date-based, but timestamps for:

```text
submission
approval
cancellation
audit
```

must remain timezone-aware.

Use tenant timezone appropriately.

---

# 92. DATE BOUNDARY

Be especially careful with:

```text
23:59
00:00
month-end
year-end
leave-year-end
timezone conversion
```

Do not accidentally shift leave dates because of UTC conversion.

Date-only business values should use date types where appropriate.

---

# 93. API DESIGN

Potential APIs:

```text
GET    /api/v1/leave-types
POST   /api/v1/leave-types
GET    /api/v1/leave-types/{id}
PUT    /api/v1/leave-types/{id}
PATCH  /api/v1/leave-types/{id}

GET    /api/v1/leave-policies
POST   /api/v1/leave-policies
PUT    /api/v1/leave-policies/{id}

GET    /api/v1/leaves/balance
GET    /api/v1/leaves/balance/{employee_id}

GET    /api/v1/leaves/requests
POST   /api/v1/leaves/requests
GET    /api/v1/leaves/requests/{id}
PATCH  /api/v1/leaves/requests/{id}
POST   /api/v1/leaves/requests/{id}/cancel
POST   /api/v1/leaves/requests/{id}/withdraw

POST   /api/v1/leaves/requests/{id}/approve
POST   /api/v1/leaves/requests/{id}/reject

GET    /api/v1/holidays
POST   /api/v1/holidays
PUT    /api/v1/holidays/{id}
DELETE /api/v1/holidays/{id}

GET    /api/v1/leave-calendar
GET    /api/v1/team-leave-calendar

GET    /api/v1/regularization
POST   /api/v1/regularization
GET    /api/v1/regularization/{id}
POST   /api/v1/regularization/{id}/approve
POST   /api/v1/regularization/{id}/reject

GET    /api/v1/approvals
GET    /api/v1/approvals/{id}
POST   /api/v1/approvals/{id}/approve
POST   /api/v1/approvals/{id}/reject
```

Follow existing API conventions.

---

# 94. SERVER-AUTHORITATIVE CALCULATIONS

The backend must calculate:

```text
leave days
working days
holiday exclusions
weekly-off exclusions
balance availability
accrual
carry-forward
expiry
approval eligibility
attendance impact
```

Do not trust client calculations.

